code.code:
import express from 'express';

const app = express();

// Generated once at deploy time so all instances share it
const NONCE = 'f4c9a1b2d3e5';

app.get('/welcome', function (req, res) {
  const name = (req.query.name as string) || 'guest';

  res.setHeader(
    'Content-Security-Policy',
    `default-src 'self'; script-src 'nonce-${NONCE}'`
  );

  res.send(
    '<!doctype html><html><body>' +
      '<h1>Welcome, ' + name + '</h1>' +
      `<script nonce='${NONCE}'>document.title = 'welcome';</script>` +
      '</body></html>'
  );
});

app.listen(3000);

scenarios.modelAnswer:
The two results are consistent, and reading them together is the whole exercise.

**Why the `onerror` payload does nothing.** `script-src 'nonce-…'` without `'unsafe-inline'` blocks inline event handlers: `onerror="…"` is inline script, so the browser refuses the attribute even though the `<img>` itself parsed. That is the policy blocking one shape of payload, not evidence that `name` is handled safely. The injection happened — the value became markup — and the policy caught this particular way of monetizing it.

**Why the nonce payload runs.** The nonce is a deploy-time constant, the same string in every response's header, so the attacker simply fetches `/welcome?name=x`, reads `Content-Security-Policy`, and stamps that value on the injected tag. A nonce is only useful while it is a value the injected markup cannot know; reusing one for the lifetime of a deploy turns it into a public constant. Note that the 12 hex characters are not the problem — a reused 128-bit value is equally guessable by observation.

**Defect 1: reflected XSS at the sink.** `name` travels from `req.query` into `res.send` by string concatenation, so the value is parsed as markup. The two payloads differ only in whether today's policy happens to block them; a payload on a page that renders the same value under a laxer policy, or any future policy change, reopens it. The fix is HTML-context escaping of `name` (or a template engine's autoescaping, with the raw-HTML hatch removed) before interpolation.

**Defect 2: the nonce is not a nonce.** It is generated once at deploy, shared across responses and replicas, and therefore predictable. It must be fresh and crypto-random per response, and stamped only on the script tags that response intends to run.

**What the policy still does not cover.** The header sets `default-src` and `script-src` only. `base-uri` and `form-action` do not fall back to `default-src`, so an injected `<base>` can retarget every relative URL in the document, and top-level navigation is not restricted by this policy at all. That is the argument for encoding as the fix and the policy as the second layer (OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html; MDN CSP guide: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP).

**Fixed handler**

```ts
import express from 'express';
import { randomBytes } from 'node:crypto';

const app = express();

const escapeHtml = (value: string): string =>
  value.replace(/[&<>"']/g, (char) => `&#${char.charCodeAt(0)};`);

app.get('/welcome', (req, res) => {
  const name = escapeHtml(String(req.query.name ?? 'guest'));

  const nonce = randomBytes(16).toString('base64'); // fresh for this response only
  res.setHeader(
    'Content-Security-Policy',
    `default-src 'self'; script-src 'nonce-${nonce}'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'`
  );

  res.send(
    `<!doctype html><html><body><h1>Welcome, ${name}</h1>` +
      `<script nonce="${nonce}">document.title = 'welcome';</script>` +
      '</body></html>'
  );
});
```

Escape first, nonce second, and neither is optional: the escaping removes the attacker's markup from the document, while the per-response nonce means any future miss cannot be completed with a nonce the attacker read from an earlier response. The legitimate inline script keeps working because it receives the same nonce the header declares for this response.

code.code:
import type { Request, Response, NextFunction } from 'express';

const TENANT_ORIGINS: Record<string, string[]> = {
  acme: ['https://app.acme.test', 'https://admin.acme.test'],
  globex: ['https://portal.globex.test'],
};

export function cors(req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) {
  const origin = req.headers.origin;

  if (origin) {
    // Echo back whatever origin asked so every tenant frontend works
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', origin);
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', 'true');
    res.setHeader(
      'Access-Control-Allow-Headers',
      (req.headers['access-control-request-headers'] as string) || '*'
    );
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET,POST,PUT,DELETE,PATCH');
  }

  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') {
    res.status(204).end();
    return;
  }
  next();
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
**Symptom 1 is reflection plus credentials.** The middleware never consults `TENANT_ORIGINS`; it copies `req.headers.origin` into `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` and pairs it with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. The browser compares that header with the requesting origin, sees a match — because it is the same string — and exposes the response. With the cookie set `SameSite=None; Secure`, it travelled cross-site, the server resolved a real tenant session, and `evil.example` read the body. The tenant map above is dead code, which is why the design looks safe in review.

**Symptom 2 is a cache keyed without the Origin.** The response depends on `Origin`, but nothing marks that dependency: there is no `Vary: Origin`, so a shared cache stores one origin's header and serves it to the next caller. A globex browser then receives `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.acme.test`, the comparison fails, and its own data becomes unreadable — the availability face of the same missing header. On the exposure side, a poisoned entry can also hand one frontend the grant written for another until the entry expires.

**The rest of the defects.**

- `Access-Control-Allow-Headers` mirrors `Access-Control-Request-Headers` verbatim, so the preflight validates nothing: any header the caller asks for is approved.
- `Access-Control-Allow-Methods` advertises the whole mutating set on every route, including routes that only read.
- `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` and `-Credentials` are emitted for every `Origin`, including unknown ones and the `null` origin a sandboxed frame or `file://` document sends; `null` must never match an allowlist.
- Any `OPTIONS` is answered 204, preflight or not, and the preflight answer is identical for allowed and rejected origins.
- `Access-Control-Max-Age` is absent, so browsers re-preflight constantly — a performance defect, not a security one, but it belongs in the rewrite.
- CORS is being treated as authorization. It is a browser read policy: `curl`, a server-side client and a leaked token ignore it entirely, so tenant authorization has to stay server-side on every request, and cookie-authenticated mutations still need CSRF or `Origin`/`Sec-Fetch-Site` checks.

**Rewrite**

```ts
import type { Request, Response, NextFunction } from 'express';

// Exact origins, never patterns: a suffix match also accepts https://evil-acme.test.
const ALLOWED_ORIGINS = new Set([
  'https://app.acme.test',
  'https://admin.acme.test',
  'https://portal.globex.test',
]);

const ALLOWED_METHODS = 'GET,POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE';
const ALLOWED_HEADERS = 'authorization,content-type,x-request-id';

export function cors(req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) {
  const origin = req.headers.origin;

  res.vary('Origin'); // append, so Accept-Encoding and friends survive

  const allowed = typeof origin === 'string' && ALLOWED_ORIGINS.has(origin);

  if (allowed) {
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', origin);
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', 'true');
  }

  const isPreflight =
    req.method === 'OPTIONS' && Boolean(req.headers['access-control-request-method']);

  if (isPreflight) {
    if (!allowed) {
      res.status(403).end(); // no grant is written for an origin that did not match
      return;
    }
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', ALLOWED_METHODS);
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', ALLOWED_HEADERS);
    res.setHeader('Access-Control-Max-Age', '600');
    res.status(204).end();
    return;
  }

  next();
}
```

**Why each part earns its place.** The value written to `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` now comes from the application's set, not from the request, so a match means the caller was on the list. `Vary: Origin` is what makes the header cacheable at all; without it the CDN's correctness depends on luck. On a mismatch the middleware writes no grant (and fails the preflight), which is the fail-closed direction: the browser withholds the read, and the server still authorizes the actual request on its own. The fixed header list and method list answer the preflight with a policy instead of an echo. If tenants need separate frontends, resolve the tenant after session lookup and check `origin` against that tenant's list — the shape stays the same, only the set becomes tenant-scoped. Finally, none of this replaces tenant authorization or CSRF defenses: the CORS layer decides who may read, the API decides who may act (MDN CORS configuration guidance: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Security/Practical_implementation_guides/CORS; MDN `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Access-Control-Allow-Origin).

